<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Gateway - the system's sole ingress and composition root.

    ROLE
      Gateway is the only service external clients reach. It publishes the REST surface of contract
      C-09 (/health, /v1/ping, /v1/capabilities and the /v1/datawindow/** projections of C-03 and
      C-04), re-expresses the legacy framework lifecycle as ASP.NET Core host startup and shutdown,
      and declares the four reserved Phase 2 extension points as routing metadata. REST rather than
      gRPC at this boundary is a deliberate per service transport decision: an ingress needs browser
      and third party reach, HTTP standard caching and proxying, and mature OpenAPI tooling, all of
      which gRPC-Web would forfeit by requiring a translating proxy.

    LEGACY REFERENCE (read only: never edited, never built, never shipped)
      The framework's PowerBuilder application object is the composition root reference. Its open
      event pairs the framework initialize call with the finalize call in its close event, hardcodes
      the locale to "en", and its system error event unpacks a seven field assert payload before
      terminating the process. Those behaviours are reproduced by the sources under Composition/,
      Configuration/ and Diagnostics/, and this manifest exists to give those sources their compile
      time dependencies.

      Nothing in the read only legacy tree is copied, embedded or globbed by this project: it
      declares no Content, None or EmbeddedResource item at all, and no path in it points anywhere
      near that tree. In particular no item brings the repository root localization resource table
      (pfw.i18n.xml) into the output, because the localization library opens that file by relative
      filename at run time and its absence is exactly what produces the preserved silent passthrough
      behaviour. Materialising it would be a behavioural change dressed up as completeness.

      The two legacy PowerBuilder project objects were read as reference for build INTENT only and
      are deliberately not translated. They disagree with each other on library count (29 versus 28
      entries) and on vendor, one of them embeds developer workstation absolute paths and a retired
      library name, and both name an image codec library that exists nowhere in the repository, so
      neither would build as written. No version, library list or vendor string in this .NET build
      graph derives from either of them; every version is pinned centrally at the repository root.

    WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SET
      TargetFramework, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors,
      EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel and ManagePackageVersionsCentrally all arrive from the
      repository root Directory.Build.props through MSBuild directory walk up. Restating any of them
      here would create a second source of truth that could silently drift. OutputType is left at
      the Web SDK default of Exe; only the sibling test project needs to set it explicitly, and that
      is a different file. InvariantGlobalization is likewise left unset, because the ported
      localization surface needs ICU to stay live.

      Every PackageReference below is VERSIONLESS. Central Package Management is enabled at the
      repository root, so versions live solely in Directory.Packages.props; a Version attribute here
      would be a build error and would let one service drift off a mandatory security pin.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>PowerFramework.Gateway</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Gateway</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly four project references, and exactly these four.

      PowerFramework.Contracts            The published boundary: the generated gRPC client for
                                          dataservices.v1 plus the OpenAPI definitions. This is the
                                          ONLY coupling Gateway has towards another service, and it
                                          is a contract definition rather than a shared behaviour
                                          back door.
      PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel        The return code algebra and Enums, including the eight
                                          INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_* capability bits that
                                          Composition/CapabilityFlags.cs projects as configuration.
      PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics   The assert payload, stack trace and caller info types that
                                          Diagnostics/SystemErrorHandler.cs reproduces.
      PowerFramework.Shared.Localization  The I18N surface and locale providers behind the preserved
                                          "en" default in Configuration/GatewayOptions.cs.

    Not referenced, on purpose:
      * PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful and PowerFramework.Shared.Containers. Gateway consumes
        neither. The event broker belongs to DataServices, and the ordered map and vector belong to
        DataServices and Persistence.
      * Any sibling service project. Gateway must restore, build and test standalone from a clean
        checkout, so a reference to PowerFramework.DataServices, PowerFramework.Persistence or
        PowerFramework.Security is never permitted: it would collapse the decomposition this
        refactor exists to produce. Gateway reaches those services over the network, through the
        contracts project above.
      * Anything backing a deferred capability. DesignSystem, Documents, Integration and
        ScriptBridge have no project and no package anywhere in this build graph. The four reserved
        routes are route table metadata inside Endpoints/DeferredCapabilityEndpoints.cs, and nothing
        compiles behind them.

    Paths are relative and rise exactly three levels (PowerFramework.Gateway, then gateway-service,
    then services) to reach the repository root, which is also the Docker build context of the
    sibling Dockerfile. No path in this file assumes the build context is this directory.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Shared libraries and the published contract boundary">
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics/PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Localization/PowerFramework.Shared.Localization.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="REST ingress and its OpenAPI document (contract C-09)">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" />
    <!--
      Microsoft.OpenApi is referenced DIRECTLY and deliberately, even though
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi already pulls it in, because a direct reference is what binds the
      mandatory 2.11.0 pin held in the repository root Directory.Packages.props.

      The transitive version is 2.0.0, which raises NuGet audit warning NU1903 at high severity.
      Moving forward to the 3.x line instead BREAKS the build: the 10.0.x OpenAPI source generator
      is compiled against the 2.x object model and emits two CS0200 "cannot assign, read only"
      errors from its own generated XML comment support file. 2.11.0 is the highest published 2.x
      and the only value that is simultaneously non vulnerable and compatible. Do not relax this,
      and do not add a Version attribute to move it: change the central pin or nothing.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Inbound JWT validation (/health anonymous; /v1/ping is 401 untokened)">
    <!--
      Validation only. Security is the sole token issuer in the system, so the token minting package
      that Security alone references (see the authentication group in the repository root
      Directory.Packages.props) is deliberately absent from this project. Gateway holds verification
      material fetched from Security's published JWKS document and no signing authority of its own,
      so referencing any minting library here would be a structural contradiction even if no code
      ever used it.

      Health check registration ships inside the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework, so
      /health and Gateway's aggregation of its three upstreams need no package reference at all.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="gRPC client for DataServices (client side only; Gateway hosts no gRPC service)">
    <!--
      Grpc.AspNetCore carries Grpc.AspNetCore.Server.ClientFactory, so the typed clients under
      Clients/ need no further package. The protocol definitions themselves live in
      PowerFramework.Contracts, which generates both the client and the server stubs, so this
      project declares no Protobuf items of its own. The gRPC server reflection package is
      deliberately absent as well: it is a convenience for command line gRPC tooling rather than a
      requirement, and Gateway hosts no gRPC service for it to reflect over.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Grpc.AspNetCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Resilience on the network edges that decomposition itself creates">
    <!--
      This package is required BY the transition rather than layered on top of it, and the reasoning
      is recorded here so it cannot be mistaken for scope creep against the minimal change clause.

      An in process call cannot fail in transit; a network call can. Gateway's calls to DataServices
      and Security are edges the legacy framework simply did not have, because PowerFramework was an
      in process library with no process of its own, no listener and no server tier. Without retry
      and circuit breaking, the first transient network fault would surface as a defect the legacy
      could not have had, which is a regression introduced by the refactor rather than a preserved
      behaviour. No latency, throughput or availability claim is made or implied by its presence:
      the repository publishes no such budget, so none may be asserted.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
